  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final answer. Need detailed technical tutorial. Must use complete sentences? Probably all sentences complete. Avoid repeating. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, examples, tradeoffs, performance pitfalls. Need probably structure with sections. Need concrete examples SQL and maybe pseudo plans. Need explain RDBMS query execution: tokenizer/parser, resolver, optimizer, planner, execution engine, access paths, indexes, join algorithms, transactions/locking/MVCC, result return. Need tradeoffs: B-tree vs hash, indexes write cost, covering indexes, statistics, optimizer assumptions, index nested loop vs hash merge, join order, materialization, common pitfalls: implicit type conversion, functions on indexed columns, select *, unindexed columns, missing stats, N+1, correlated subqueries, large transactions, lock contention, autocommit, isolation levels, index fragmentation, etc.\n\nNeed avoid repeating. Could write as tutorial with examples. Need maybe use PostgreSQL/MySQL-like SQL generic. Complete sentences. We can include diagrams? text.\n\nLet's plan final:\n\nTitle: How a relational database executes a SQL query.\n\nIntro: A relational database engine transforms a textual SQL statement into a series of low-level operations. Example schema: customers(id PK, region), orders(id PK, customer_id FK, status, total, created_at), order_items... indexes.\n\n1. Parsing and validation\n- lexical analysis tokens\n- parse tree/AST based grammar\n- name resolution: catalogs, schemas, table/column names\n- semantic checks: types, functions, expressions\n- example: SELECT c.name, o.total FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON c.id=o.customer_id WHERE o.status='shipped';\n- If typo -> error.\n- prepared statements parse once, plan cache.\n\n2. Query rewriting / simplification\n- constants folding, predicate pushdown? Some done in planner.\n- view expansion, common subexpressions, subquery decorrelation.\n- example: WHERE status='shipped' AND status IN ('shipped','pending') maybe simplified? maybe not.\n\n3. Optimization/planning\n- cost-based optimizer estimates cardinalities using statistics (row counts, histograms, NDV",
